tests/fixtures/
---------------

These files are recordings of the reference implementation's behaviour, made by
running the real `tatr` binary over the 79 tasks of its own repository:

  tsoding-tatr-raw.json   the complete text of those 79 TASK.md files
  tatr-ls-output.json     their ids, titles, statuses, priorities and tags
  tsoding-tatr.json       the same fields, from a second invocation
  tatr-graph-edges.json   the task ids they cite, as pairs
  tql-cli-cases.json      50 queries, and the ids the CLI printed for each
  tatr-query-errors.json  invalid queries, and the diagnostics the CLI printed

The task text, the titles and the diagnostics in them are the work of the
authors of tatr <https://github.com/tsoding/tatr> and are covered by that
project's licence, the GNU General Public License version 2. They are kept here
as test data, and they earn their place: the suite replays them field by field,
so a divergence between this viewer and the reference implementation fails the
build rather than reaching a reader.

Two smaller quotations, for completeness. src/lib/tatr/board.ts quotes one
sentence of an upstream task in a comment, as the reason a decision was made;
the README quotes upstream typos to explain why repository content is shown
verbatim. Both are attributed where they appear.


What is not borrowed
--------------------

No source code from tatr is copied or translated into this repository. This
viewer is an independent implementation, in TypeScript, of the same file format
and the same query language — a format and a behaviour rather than an
expression. Where a comment names a file of the C source, it says where the
behaviour is defined, not where the code came from; what holds the two together
is the differential tests above.

Two exceptions, both quotations rather than code, and both deliberate: the
diagnostics in src/lib/tql/query.ts reproduce word for word what the CLI prints
above a bad query, because a query is meant to move between the two tools
unchanged and so is the complaint about it. They are credited where they
appear.
